  "query_preview": "(Today through Saturday)...\nIssued at 205 AM EDT Sun Oct 18 2026\n\nCurrent observations show low 30s to mid 30s dew points across \nthe area of which matches up with the ongoing forecast of near to \nbelow freezing temperatures over N/NE portions of central Indiana \nand widespread frost over the remainder of the area. The one caveat \nto this is E/SE portions of the Freeze Warning where current dew \npoints of 35-37. \n\nThe surface high will push off to the east on throughout the day, \nallowing for weak S/SE flow to develop over the Ohio Valley by this \nafternoon. This along with mostly clear skies will lead to a slight \nrebound in temperatures with highs on Sunday expected to be in the \nlow 60s. Clear skies and dry surface conditions will continue large \ndiurnal swings, but there will be enough of an air mass change to \nkeep temperatures above freezing Sunday night into Monday morning. \nFrost potential will remain high over NE portions of the area, but \nheadlines will depend if any hard freezes occur this morning.\n\nUpper level riding will continue to build throughout the beginning \nof next week leading to subsidence and dry conditions. Continued S/SW \nflow west of the surface high will maintain the steady warming trend \nearly next week as well. 850mb temperatures are expected to increase \nabove 10C by Tuesday. This along with dry adiabatic lapse rates in \nthe PBL should lead to daily temperature above seasonal normals \nMonday through Wednesday. Expect large diurnal swings to continue \nwith lows in the 40s.\n\nBy later in the week, mid to low level northerly flow upstream of a \nbuilding ridge will likely curtail daily temperature gains. This\nshould maintain highs in the low 70s into next weekend. Subsidence\nwill remain high with continued large diurnal temperature swings \nand dry conditions.",
